# No Realistic-Looking Example Secrets

When writing or editing request bodies, response examples, headers, or any
other sample data in collection YAML (`*.request.yaml`, `*.example.yaml`,
`definition.yaml`, etc.):

- Never use values that look like real secrets for fields such as tokens,
  session IDs, API keys, access tokens, passwords, or similar credentials
  (e.g. `request_token`, `session_id`, `guest_session_id`, `access_token`,
  `api_key`). This includes hex strings (e.g. 32/40-char hashes), JWT-shaped
  strings (`xxx.yyy.zzz`), and other high-entropy, plausible-looking values —
  even when copied verbatim from a provider's own public docs. These trigger
  secret scanners (e.g. Gitleaks, GitHub secret scanning) and are
  indistinguishable from leaked credentials at a glance.
- Use an obvious, low-entropy placeholder instead, matching the field name,
  e.g. `<session_id>`, `<request_token>`, `<guest_session_id>`,
  `<v4_access_token>`. This mirrors the existing `<API Key>` convention
  already used for `Authorization` header values in this repo.
- Non-credential identifiers that happen to be hex/hash-shaped but are not
  secrets (e.g. a public Gravatar hash embedded in an `avatar_path` URL, a
  review ID) do not need to be replaced — only replace values in
  auth/credential-shaped fields.
- When adding a new collection generated from an OpenAPI spec or copied from
  provider documentation, scan request/example bodies for this pattern before
  committing and replace any realistic-looking token/secret examples with
  placeholders.
